INT. COMMUNITY CENTER KITCHEN - MORNING

A big institutional kitchen, steel counters, a dozen mismatched pots. Flour on every surface. A banner on the wall: "WESTBROOK BAKE-OFF - 40TH ANNUAL."

GLORIA ESPINOSA-REED (50s, apron, reading glasses on a chain) kneads dough with ferocity. Her daughter MAYA (16, headphones around her neck) arranges cooling racks. Her son TOBY (8, a paper chef hat too big for him) licks a spoon.

At the far counter, GLORIA'S HUSBAND, DENNIS REED (50s, mild, a man who apologizes to furniture), is measuring sugar with great care.

GLORIA
Dennis, level it off.

DENNIS
I am leveling it.

GLORIA
You're mounding it.

DENNIS
It's a gentle mound.

MAYA
Dad, it's a hill.

TOBY
Can I have the spoon?

GLORIA
You already have the spoon.

TOBY
Can I have another spoon?

Dennis hands him one. Gloria sighs, but she is smiling.

The wall phone rings. Maya answers it.

MAYA
Espinosa-Reed residence, kitchen division.

JUDGE'S ASSISTANT (V.O.)
(over phone)
Good morning. This is the contest office. We're confirming your entry for ten o'clock.

MAYA
Yes. Cinnamon pull-apart.

JUDGE'S ASSISTANT (V.O.)
Wonderful. And the family recipe is original?

MAYA
(glancing at Gloria)
Totally original.

JUDGE'S ASSISTANT (V.O.)
Perfect. We'll see you shortly.

Maya hangs up. Gloria is watching her.

GLORIA
Why did you say it like that?

MAYA
Like what?

GLORIA
Like you were reading it off a card.

MAYA
I wasn't.

GLORIA
Maya.

MAYA
Mom, it's fine.

GLORIA
Is the recipe original?

MAYA
Mom.

GLORIA
Maya Rosalind.

MAYA
It's Grandma Inez's recipe. It's practically ours.

GLORIA
Inez's recipe is from the Hartwell Cookbook, Maya. Page ninety-one. Mrs. Hartwell is a judge.

MAYA
I know.

GLORIA
You know?

MAYA
I knew last week. I looked it up. I didn't want to say anything.

GLORIA
You didn't want to say anything.

MAYA
You looked so happy.

Dennis sets down the sugar scoop very quietly. Toby stops licking his spoon.

GLORIA
I made this recipe for thirty years. My mother gave it to me. She said her mother wrote it herself.

MAYA
Maybe she did. Maybe she wrote it down from the cookbook.

GLORIA
Maybe.

DENNIS
Gloria.

GLORIA
(sharp)
Dennis, not now.

DENNIS
I only meant, maybe change a little. Add the cardamom.

GLORIA
You want me to add the cardamom.

DENNIS
It's the one thing that isn't in the cookbook.

A long silence. The oven timer ticks.

GLORIA
Maya, hand me the cardamom.

Maya blinks. Then she crosses to the spice rack and hands over a small tin.

The door swings open. MRS. PRUDENCE HARTWELL (70s, lavender cardigan, a clipboard held like a scepter) enters, trailed by an assistant.

PRUDENCE
Gloria, dear, I came early to see the kitchens. Is that cinnamon I smell?

GLORIA
(brightly)
It is.

PRUDENCE
Is it the pull-apart?

GLORIA
It is.

PRUDENCE
I used to make one just like it. My own grandmother's recipe. I published it, you know.

Gloria's hand tightens around the cardamom tin.

GLORIA
I did not know that.

Prudence leans over the dough, sniffs, and smiles.

PRUDENCE
Oh, there's something different in this. Something warm. What is it?

Maya, Dennis, and Toby all look at Gloria.

GLORIA
Cardamom.

PRUDENCE
Lovely. Page ninety-one, if I'm not mistaken. But with your own touch.

Gloria goes completely still. Prudence pats her arm and sweeps out.

The door closes. Nobody speaks.

TOBY
Does that mean we win?

Gloria laughs. A short, startled laugh, then a longer one. She wipes her eyes with a floury wrist.

GLORIA
It means we bake.
